Two app_server fixes that make CSS opacity work

1. BoundingBoxCallbacks::SetFontShear takes the wrong units

A BPicture stores the font shear as (degrees - 90) in radians:

    WriteSetFontShear((font.Shear() - 90) * (M_PI / 180))   ServerPicture.cpp

so a font with no shear at all is stored as 0.0. CanvasCallbacks::SetFontShear
converts it back; BoundingBoxCallbacks::SetFontShear did not, and set the draw
state's font to a shear of 0 degrees. ServerFont::EmbeddedTransformation then
computes

    transform.ShearBy(B_ORIGIN, (90.0 - 0) * M_PI / 180.0, 0.0)

which is tan(pi/2) = 1.6e16 in the matrix's shx. Every glyph x that comes out
of the transform is then around 1e17 while y stays correct, because shx only
feeds x:

    *x = x * sx + y * shx + tx

Measured in the guest, with the transform printed straight out of
ServerFont::EmbeddedTransformation:

    VPROBE shear=0 rot=0 size=20000 -> sx=1000 shy=0 shx=huge sy=1000
    VPROBE   raw2 x=59 y=-93      the serialized glyph vertex, fine
    VPROBE   cv2  x=0.921 y=-1.453    after conv_curve, still fine
    VPROBE   to2  x=-2.3e17 y=-1.453  after conv_transform

The first two vertices of a glyph survive because they sit on the baseline,
where y is 0 and shx has nothing to multiply.

2. BoundingBoxCallbacks::DrawStringLocations was an empty "// TODO"

Every other drawing command contributes to the bounding box a BPicture is
measured with; this one contributed nothing.

That matters for layers. BView::BeginLayer records into a picture, and
Layer::RenderToBitmap measures that picture with PictureBoundingBoxPlayer to
decide how large a bitmap to render it into. If the box comes out invalid,
RenderToBitmap returns NULL and Canvas::BlendLayer returns without drawing
anything at all.

WebKit draws all of its text through
BView::DrawString(const char*, const BPoint[], int32) -- FontHaiku.cpp builds
an offset per glyph and calls that -- and CSS opacity below 1 is implemented
with BeginLayer. So in WebPositive every element with opacity < 1 disappeared:
a layer holding only text got an invalid box and was dropped whole, and a
layer holding text plus one other shape got a box covering only the shape.
example.com is the second case; its <div> has opacity 0.8, and all that
appeared was the underline of the link.

Neither of these is arm64-specific. They are only conspicuous here because
this port has no other browser to notice them with, and the first one hides
the second: without the shear fix, ServerFont::GetBoundingBoxes returns a
garbage "left" and the exact per-glyph boxes this implementation uses cannot
be trusted.

Verified in WebPositive on arm64: a page of divs at opacity 1, 0.99, 0.8 and
0.5 draws all four rows at the right shades, and example.com renders its
heading, body text and link. Without these, only the link underline appears.
See screenshots/css-opacity-layers.png and
screenshots/example-com-opacity-fixed.png.

--- a/src/servers/app/PictureBoundingBoxPlayer.cpp
+++ b/src/servers/app/PictureBoundingBoxPlayer.cpp
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include "PictureBoundingBoxPlayer.h"
 
 #include <new>
+#include <math.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 
 #include "DrawState.h"
@@ -697,7 +698,16 @@ void
 BoundingBoxCallbacks::SetFontShear(float shear)
 {
 	ServerFont font;
-	font.SetShear(shear);
+	// The picture stores the shear as (degrees - 90) in radians -- see
+	// PictureDataWriter's WriteSetFontShear in ServerPicture.cpp -- so a font
+	// with no shear at all arrives here as 0.0. Setting that as the shear in
+	// degrees, which is what this used to do, leaves the draw state's font at
+	// 0 degrees, and ServerFont::EmbeddedTransformation then computes
+	// tan((90 - 0) degrees), which is 1.6e16. Every glyph x coordinate in the
+	// bounding box comes out around 1e17 after that, while y stays correct,
+	// because the skew only feeds x. CanvasCallbacks::SetFontShear already
+	// converts; this is the same conversion.
+	font.SetShear(shear * (180 / M_PI) + 90);
 	fState->GetDrawState()->SetFont(font, B_FONT_SHEAR);
 }
 
@@ -895,9 +905,60 @@ void
 BoundingBoxCallbacks::DrawStringLocations(const char* string, size_t length,
 	const BPoint locations[], size_t locationCount)
 {
-	// TODO
-}
+	TRACE_BB("%p string locations (%ld locations)\n", fState, locationCount);
+
+	// Without this, text drawn with per-glyph locations contributes nothing to
+	// a layer's bounding box. A layer holding only such text then gets an
+	// invalid box, Layer::RenderToBitmap returns NULL, and the whole layer is
+	// dropped; a layer holding text plus one other shape gets a box covering
+	// only the shape. That is what made every element with opacity < 1
+	// invisible in WebPositive, which draws all of its text through
+	// BView::DrawString(const char*, const BPoint[], int32).
+
+	if (locationCount == 0 || string == NULL || length == 0)
+		return;
+
+	ServerFont font = fState->GetDrawState()->Font();
+
+	int32 numChars = (int32)locationCount;
+	BStackOrHeapArray<BRect, 64> boxes(numChars);
+	if (!boxes.IsValid())
+		return;
 
+	// A glyph with no outline -- a space -- is reported through
+	// ConsumeEmptyGlyph, which writes nothing into the array, so the entries
+	// have to start out recognisably empty or the union picks up whatever the
+	// stack held.
+	for (int32 i = 0; i < numChars; i++)
+		boxes[i] = BRect(0, 0, -1, -1);
+
+	escapement_delta delta = { 0.0, 0.0 };
+	if (font.GetBoundingBoxes(string, (int32)length, numChars, boxes, false,
+			B_SCREEN_METRIC, delta, false) != B_OK) {
+		return;
+	}
+
+	// The boxes come back relative to each glyph's own origin.
+	BRect rect(0, 0, -1, -1);
+	for (int32 i = 0; i < numChars; i++) {
+		if (!boxes[i].IsValid())
+			continue;
+		BRect box = boxes[i].OffsetByCopy(locations[i]);
+		rect = rect.IsValid() ? (rect | box) : box;
+	}
+	if (!rect.IsValid())
+		return;
+
+	// A pixel of slack for antialiasing, which reaches outside the glyph box.
+	rect.InsetBy(-1, -1);
+
+	fState->PenToLocalTransform().Apply(&rect);
+	fState->IncludeRect(rect);
+
+	BPoint location = locations[locationCount - 1];
+	fState->PenToLocalTransform().Apply(&location);
+	fState->GetDrawState()->SetPenLocation(location);
+}
 
 void
 BoundingBoxCallbacks::SetFillRule(int32 fillRule)
